AB  - Mental health sentiment classification plays a critical role in the detection of psychological distress and early interventions. Social media text tends to be informal, domain-specific and representative of subtle emotional expressions. It will become a challenging to any classification model. Current models, integrate Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) because BERT contextualized embeddings and LSTM sequential modeling were appealing. In mental health applications, BERT + LSTM has significant disadvantages such as vulnerability to class imbalance, token fragmentation of domain-specific phrases, circumscribed use of emotional and psycholinguistic indicators and context reduction for lengthy posts. In order to overcome these challenges, a Psycholinguistic Emotion-Aware Reconstruction Learning for Mental Health (PEARL-MH) is proposed for multi-class mental health sentiment classification. PEARL-MH brings in: (1) Contextualized Psychological Augmentation (CPA) for creating label-aware text variants of minority classes without compromising significant symptom cues; (2) Symptom-Phrase Graph Tokenization (SPGT) to dynamically recognize and maintain domain-specific multi-word expressions; (3) a Dual-Channel Cognitive-Emotive Encoder (DCEE) that combines symptom-graph meanings with valence arousal dominance emotion arcs and (4) Diagnosis-Aware Capsule Routing (DACR) for hierarchical prediction of labels based on mental health taxonomy. An Emotional Margin Contrastive Loss (EMCL) provides separation between distant classes and congregates similar classes. PEARL-MH is implemented on a hand-picked multi-source dataset for seven mental health conditions such as Normal, Depression, Suicidal, Anxiety, Stress, Bipolar and Personality Disorder. PEARL-MH achieves an accuracy of 95.4%, macro-F1 of 94.7% and ROC-AUC of 0.98. It shows a significant improvement over the baseline model BERT + LSTM (accuracy of 70.0%, macro-F1 of 66.0%) and even over strong transformer models like RoBERTa-Large (accuracy of 90.8%). The model shows a significant improvement in the detection of the minority class with Bipolar and Suicidal F1-scores improving from 0.47 and 0.57 to 0.91 and 0.94, respectively.
