AB  - Onboard estimation of lithium-ion battery state-of-health (SOH) is essential for safe electric-vehicle operation but is complicated by nonlinear capacity fade, data scarcity, and local regeneration. This paper presents LMM-PatchTSSA, a lightweight framework that couples a Local Mixing Module (LMM) with a Patch Time Series Transformer (PatchTST). The LMM extracts short-term, degradation-sensitive signatures from charge–discharge curves, revealing early-stage aging indicators that standard transformers overlook. PatchTST adopts Token-Statistics Self-Attention (TSSA) using second-order statistics, reducing complexity from quadratic to linear while capturing long-range dependencies. A frozen-backbone transfer strategy mitigates chemistry-induced domain shifts. On CALCE, the model achieves an average RMSE of 0.0118. In zero-shot transfer from CALCE (NMC) to NASA (LCO) cells, R-squared exceeds 0.95, reaching 0.9913 after minimal fine-tuning. External validation on the recently released TBSI-Sunwoda dataset confirms generalizability, with R-squared of 0.9919 and RMSE of 0.0085 under a same-temperature hold-out protocol. These results demonstrate accurate SOH prediction, robust cross-chemistry generalization and low inference overhead, making the framework suitable for real-time battery management systems.
