KW  - Critical discourse analysis
KW  - Multimodality
KW  - Textbooks
KW  - Geopolitical imagination
KW  - Techno-nationalism
KW  - Mental maps
KW  - Turkish as a foreign language
AB  - Language instruction extends well beyond the simple conveyance of grammatical principles. Through it, states cultivate their self-image and affirm their status within the international order; language instruction, in this sense, functions as an instrument of statecraft. Türkiye serves as a notable illustration in this context. Over the course of roughly two decades, Türkiye's cultural diplomacy has transitioned from an “EU-centered integration” goal to a paradigm of “strategic autonomy.” This paper examines how that transition has shaped Turkish teaching materials at the C1 level. The study utilizes a specialized dataset of approximately 86,000 tokens, integrating Multimodal Analysis and Corpus-Based Critical Discourse Analysis to compare C1-level textbooks produced by Türkiye's principal cultural diplomacy organizations: the Yunus Emre Institute (YEI) and the Türkiye Maarif Foundation (TMV). The findings point to a conceptual divergence between the two corpora. In the YEI corpus, Europe serves as an implicit reference point, while the student is largely positioned as a consumer within the global market. The TMV corpus, by contrast, articulates a different geopolitical outlook: it foregrounds technology, production, digitalisation and migration, and frames the student as a “techno-nationalist” producer rather than a consumer. The divergence, however, is carried by theme rather than by geography: place-names are if anything denser in the YEI corpus, so what sets TMV apart is the producer and migration axis. The visual evidence reinforces this pattern. Sites of production and ideals of community replace the “tourist gaze” and consumption spaces that characterize YEI. The paper argues that textbooks can be read as instruments of national identity construction that reshape “mental maps” alongside shifts in Türkiye’s foreign policy discourse. Keyness (log-likelihood) analysis locates this divergence not in geographic vocabulary, which is comparable across the two books, but in a dense and statistically robust cluster of technology, production, digitalisation and migration terms that distinguishes the 2023 TMV corpus (all p < .001). A page-level analysis of the images shows the same split, while a civilisational and Silk-Road vocabulary turns out to be heavier in the 2015 YEI volume.
