AB  - Federated learning (FL) shows great potential and promise in privacy protection and data security. However, recent studies indicate that during the training process of FL, some private training data can be recovered from the shared gradient through gradient inversion attack. An in-depth study of gradient inversion attacks is imperative to ascertain the security robustness of FL and devise strategies for defense against such attacks. Among the attacks against language models, previous work tended to combine Language Model Priors (LAMP) to realize an attack structure for alternating continuous-discrete optimization, which achieves efficient token recovery. However, effective recovery of the token order and long sentences remains a challenge. In this study, an Improved LAMP (ILAMP) method is proposed, which extends the attack structure for alternating continuous-discrete optimization by combining a new Sequence Beam Search (Seq-BS) mechanism with LAMP. Compared with previous work, Seq-BS mechanism achieves high searching efficiency and solution quality in high-dimensional spaces by employing heuristic functions and beam search strategies to dynamically optimize the candidate solution set over multiple iterations. Extensive experiments are conducted on three datasets of binary text classification, and the results demonstrate that ILAMP outperforms baseline methods in the task of gradient inversion attacks against language models.
