AB  - Three-dimensional (3D) industrial defect detection is critical for manufacturing quality assurance, robotic inspection, and safety-sensitive production environments, where reliable geometric anomaly localization directly impacts throughput and system reliability. However, 3D point clouds lack dense texture cues, exhibit unordered and irregular sampling, and contain limited annotated anomalies, which makes robust localization challenging for existing methods that often depend on handcrafted descriptors or non-differentiable ordering heuristics. We propose a compact 3D anomaly detection framework composed of a Geometric Semantic-Aware Sorter (GSAS), a Mamba State-Space Adapter (MSSA), and an Attention-Based Discriminator (ABD). GSAS constructs a differentiable, surface-consistent soft-ordering of multi-layer per-patch tokens extracted from a frozen pretrained Transformer, MSSA fuses the ordered tokens to propagate long-range cross-layer context in linear time, and ABD produces slot-level anomaly scores that are reprojected to points for dense localization. Experiments on Anomaly-ShapeNet, Real3D-AD, and the newly collected RealScan-AD benchmark show that our method achieves 91.2%, 76.6%, and 78.9% point-level AUROC, respectively, while also obtaining 38.7%, 19.7%, and 20.5% point-level AUPR. In addition, the method reaches 86.8% and 78.4% object-level AUROC on Anomaly-ShapeNet and Real3D-AD, and 80.0% on RealScan-AD, outperforming the strongest compared baselines while running at approximately 17.3 FPS with a frozen backbone.
