AB  - Accurate prediction of concrete compressive strength is critical for structural safety but challenging for conventional models due to complex nonlinearities. This study proposes a hybrid framework integrating a Feature Tokenizer Transformer (FT-Transformer), Gray Wolf Optimization (GWO), and Bagging ensemble learning. The FT-Transformer utilizes Fourier embeddings and self-attention to capture high-order dependencies, while GWO optimizes hyperparameters to enhance model capacity, and a Bagging ensemble is employed to improve robustness. Experimental results on benchmark datasets show the model achieves an Coefficient of Determination (R²) of 0.9732 and a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of ≈ 1.3 MPa, significantly outperforming traditional Bagging-CART and untuned baselines. Additionally, the incorporation of engineering-oriented derived features ensures the model's interpretability aligns with domain knowledge. To facilitate real-world application, an external-to-internal temperature equivalence and maturity mapping strategy was introduced; validation on in-situ data yielded an R² of 0.8549, demonstrating the framework's applicability for construction quality monitoring and mix-design optimization.
