AB  - Recently, studies on adversarial attacks against deep learning models have been actively conducted in the fields of computer vision and autonomous driving to ensure the security and stability of deep learning-based systems. Adversarial attacks are methods of attacking artificial intelligence models by manipulating input data to induce incorrect predictions and decisions. Input transformation adversarial attack methods and gradient adjustment adversarial attack methods were analyzed to have low attack success rates (ASR) due to the difficulty in improving transferability when the target model is robust or has a large structural difference from the surrogate model. To solve the problem of reduced transferability, existing adversarial attack methods used ensemble models to generate adversarial examples, but they were designed in a limited way in ensemble models using the logits and average loss values of the models. In order to overcome the limitations of existing loss-average ensemble adversarial attacks, this paper proposes sharpness-aware gradient ensemble (SAGE) attacks, which add an adaptive-token gradient regularization (ATGR) block to the ensemble adversarial attacks that apply sharpness-aware minimization (SAM) and block shuffle and rotation (BSR) process to the gradient values of the surrogate model. The proposed SAGE adversarial attack method consists of an ensemble gradient SAM (EGSAM) process to improve the generalization ability to successfully attack black-box models and an ATGR process to improve the transferability of vision transformer surrogate models through regularization. As a result of the experiment, the proposed BSR-SAGE adversarial attack method achieved an average ASR that is 17.4%, 27.4%, 29.3%, and 19.1% higher than the existing stochastic variance reduced ensemble (SVRE), token gradient regularization (TGR), virtual dense connection (VDC), and adaptive token tuning (ATT) methods, respectively, on the ViT-b/16 & DeiT-b/16 surrogate ensemble using the ImageNet public DB. The proposed SAGE improves the ASR of existing ensemble adversarial attacks by using the fusion approach of EGSAM and ATGR blocks that average the gradients of surrogate models. SAGE contributes to improving transferability even when there is a large structural difference between models through an ensemble strategy that improves the logits and loss averaging methods. Code is available at https://github.com/IDPLab-chosun/BSR_SAGE.
