AB  - The rapid integration of Internet of Things (IoT) devices into industrial supply chains exposes embedded gateways to distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks that can destabilize latency-sensitive industrial processes. This paper introduces a hybrid Fractional-Order PID (FOPID) and Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) Token Bucket architecture implemented at the XDP (eXpress Data Path) native hook, the earliest programmable point in the Linux kernel. The FOPID controller dynamically adjusts the Token Bucket replenishment rate using real-time network throughput and traffic statistics, enabling proportional rate regulation that helps preserve legitimate bursty flows while mitigating malicious traffic. A trigger-driven PSO layer, activated only when stability thresholds are violated, tunes the five FOPID gains by minimizing the Integrated Time Absolute Error (ITAE) criterion. The optimized parameters are transmitted to the kernel-space FOPID controller via eBPF (extended Berkeley Packet Filter) maps, allowing low-latency control updates with limited runtime overhead. The control mechanism uses a fixed control-sampling period to support timely adaptation while maintaining the timing constraints of the target cyber-physical setting. Experimental results on a physical IoT warehouse testbed show that the proposed architecture improves both resilience and service stability under multi-vector attacks. Relative to the standard Token Bucket, the method reduced packet loss by 7.7–18.5%, and compared with iptables hashlimit, it lowered CPU utilization by 10–30%. Under Layer 7 flooding, it constrained P99 latency to 132.90 ms.
