AB  - Extreme weather events like cyclones, floods and severe storms can cause disturbances in radial distribution systems (RDSs) by means of feeder outages, electrical islanding, congestion in communication networks and disturbances in energy markets. To tackle these challenges, this paper proposes a blockchain-enabled cyber-physical energy management framework that integrates topology-aware blockchain sharding with a Resilience-Triggered Emergency Token Market (RT-ETM) for DER-integrated distribution systems. The framework jointly coordinates AC power-flow feasibility, DER scheduling, blockchain-enabled peer-to-peer (P2P) energy trading, and emergency energy-token allocation, which are formulated in a vector multi-objective optimization problem considering operating cost, Energy Not Served (ENS), voltage deviation, blockchain latency, and market fairness. The proposed Cyber-Physical Mountain Gazelle Optimization Algorithm (CP-MGOA) with disaster adaptive exploration, blockchain awareness, topology awareness, adaptive constraint handling, feasibility repair and diversity preservation is used to solve the resulting nonlinear optimization problem. The framework is evaluated on a modified IEEE 69-bus RDS under four representative operating scenarios, including normal operation, single-line outage, multiple-line outage and severe communication congestion. CP-MGOA is compared with PSO, GWO, DE, NSGA-II, NSGA-III, MOEA/D and SPEA2 using Pareto-front quality, Hypervolume, diversity measures, convergence characteristics and statistical tests in 30 independent runs. A controlled ablation study is also conducted to examine the incremental contributions of the proposed CP-MGOA enhancements. Results show improved operational and resilience performance of the proposed framework in the considered scenarios, whereas RT-ETM allows critical-load restoration and more balanced emergency-energy allocation. Instead of experimental validation at the protocol level, the blockchain effects are evaluated through a sensitivity analysis on a reduced-order congestion and confirmation model. Therefore, the present study offers a simulation-based evaluation of integrated cyber–physical energy management under disaster conditions and highlights the computational and validation limitations for further investigation.
