AB  - Low-light images are characterized not only by reduced illumination but also by radiance reflected from objects, resulting in uneven luminance across regions. However, existing methods apply uniform enhancement that neglects this unevenness, leading to over-smoothed textures or structural hallucinations across luminance zones. To resolve these problems, we propose the Luminance-Ranking Autoregressive (LRAR) framework, which progressively improves images from bright to dark regions. The framework consists of a predictor network and a critic network that interact through feedback, where the critic’s reliability assessment steers the predictor’s refinement within the discrete latent token space. At each iteration, the predictor network maps the low-light image or the critic’s previous output into candidate enhancement tokens, while the critic network evaluates the candidates and selectively accepts high-quality tokens to produce a partially enhanced result for the next iteration. Through this repeated procedure, LRAR enables illumination refinement from bright regions to dark regions, propagating structural references to suppress noise and preserve structural details. Experiments on LOLv1, LOLv2, and unpaired benchmarks show that LRAR achieves 26.90 dB PSNR and 35.69 FID on LOLv1, 24.28 dB PSNR and 41.29 FID on LOLv2-Real, and an average NIQE of 3.36 on unpaired benchmarks, indicating strong generalization across diverse low-light conditions.
