DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dsp.2025.105486
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1051200425005081
KW  - High resolution
KW  - Pathology image segmentation
KW  - Adaptive fitting
KW  - Token attributes
AB  - Background
High-resolution whole slide images (WSI) pathology images are typically sliced into fixed-patch tokens with specific attributes for subsequent semantic segmentation. The large size of WSI images poses significant challenges for automated segmentation, particularly in preserving the structural integrity of pathological tissues.
Purpose
This study aims to address the challenge of achieving an optimal trade-off of token attributes between patch size and overlapping size for WSI semantic segmentation. To this end, an architecture-agnostic adaptive fitting framework of token attributes is proposed. This framework is designed to dynamically adjust token attributes during the training process, maximizing the performance of various semantic segmentation models.
Methods
The proposed framework comprises three key components. First, to identify the optimal patch size and overlapping of the dataset token attributes, Gaussian fitting and polynomial fitting are respectively utilized to fit the training evaluation metrics of diverse data subsets during the self-adaptive processing stage of training. Second, since the fitted tokens generally contain local information, it is necessary to combine adjacent tokens into large ones with expanded features, so as to capture global decoding features estimated by adaptive fitting. Finally, a dual-encoder refinement network was designed to encode the decoded features and large tokens separately, in order to mutually refine and correlate adjacent semantic features.
Results
Extensive experiments were conducted on a self-built colorectal cancer dataset, where popular semantic segmentation models, namely U-Net, U-Net++, DeepLabV3+, Attention U-Net, and SCNet were used as the backbone architectures for the adaptive fitting framework. The results demonstrated that the performance of five backbone networks has been improved, and mice reached 89.71%, 90.52%, 89.25%, 90.96%, and 90.73% respectively.
Conclusions
An effective architecture-agnostic adaptive fitting framework of token attributes for the WSI semantic segmentation task is presented, which achieves optimal segmentation accuracies for existing prevalent semantic segmentation models by carefully balancing the sliced patch size and overlap size. The proposed method provides a generalizable solution that can be easily integrated into various WSI semantic segmentation pipelines, potentially facilitating more accurate and efficient digital pathology analysis. The code for this paper will be released at https://github.com/Huang-erying/WSI-SS.
