AB  - Natural language processing (NLP) studies how to make computers understand, interpret, and generate human language. It comprises natural language understanding (mapping text to structured meaning) and natural language generation (producing fluent text from data). Core steps include tokenization, normalization, part-of-speech tagging, syntactic parsing, and named-entity recognition, assembled into task-specific pipelines. Early NLP relied on symbolic, rule-based systems that encoded grammars and domain knowledge. Data-driven methods reframed language as statistical patterns. Representations such as Bag-of-Words and TF-IDF enabled classifiers like naïve Bayes, logistic regression, and support vector machines, yet they ignored word order and context. Dense word embeddings captured distributional meaning, while contextual embeddings made representations sensitive to surrounding words. Transformers marked the inflection point. Self-attention scales sequence modeling and supports pretraining at web scale. Encoder-only models excel at understanding tasks; decoder-only models learn to predict the next token and generate coherent text; encoder-decoder models handle conditional generation. Large language models (LLMs) exhibit in-context learning, enabling few-shot adaptation without weight updates. Clinical NLP benefits directly: robust information extraction from reports, support for structured reporting, literature triage, and decision support. Retrieval-augmented generation grounds outputs in external sources to improve factuality. Practical deployment requires safeguards for privacy, bias, and hallucinations, with evaluation aligned to the task: precision/recall/F1 for extraction, and human plus automated text metrics for generation. This chapter traces the path from rules to LLMs, clarifies the technical foundations, and outlines how transformer-based systems can augment expert workflows while preserving human oversight.
