AB  - Deep learning has achieved substantial progress in automatically analyzing medical images, especially in identifying skin cancer. However, there are still significant challenges in classifying dermoscopic images, such as class imbalance, the complexity of pathological features at multiple scales, and the computational burden introduced by attention modules. In order to overcome these limitations, we propose CTokSelect, which is a hybrid model combining CNN and Transformer architectures, and is specially designed for skin lesion classification. This framework uses ResNet-101 to extract the local texture and structural details of dermoscopic images, and uses Vision Transformer to model the global relationships to better represent complex lesion regions. In addition, in order to reduce redundant background tokens and modestly improve inference efficiency, we use class tokens to evaluate the attention score of spatial tokens, so as to select important tokens for the subsequent encoding stage. Furthermore, to address the challenges brought by the unbalanced distribution of medical information, we introduce the Dyn-γ FL method. Dyn-γ FL dynamically adjusts the focusing parameter γ, so that the model can better learn minority classes and difficult cases. Through experiments on the ISIC2018 dermoscopic dataset, the proposed method demonstrates better performance than existing methods across multiple evaluation metrics. These results demonstrate that it can effectively classify different types of skin lesions and provide a reliable framework for automated skin cancer diagnosis.
