AB  - Color terms extend beyond literal hue to culturally stabilized meanings, yet little is known about whether relations among metaphorical expressions correspond to visual color patterns. This study examines cross-modal alignment in 18 metaphorical lexemes derived from Mandarin huáng 黄 ‘yellow’. It compares usage-based semantic profiles from 1800 corpus instances with color profiles from 1800 associated images. The results show a modest but reliable rank-order correspondence between semantic and color-profile similarities (Spearman ρ = 0.29, Mantel p = 0.0284), whereas the linear association did not reach significance (Pearson r = 0.25, Mantel p = 0.0633). At the lexeme level, 16 of 18 local coefficients were positive, and their mean was significant (mean local ρ = 0.249, two-sided p = 0.0135). The pattern indicates that semantic and visual organization are related without being isomorphic: shared tendencies coexist with substantial variation across individual lexemes. These findings support a hybrid account of color embodiment in which perceptual associations constrain semantic organization, while repeated cultural use stabilizes, amplifies, or redirects those associations. More broadly, the study shows how corpus-based semantic profiles and image-based color statistics can illuminate graded multimodal structure within a culturally specific lexical family.
