AB  - Time-interval-aware sequential recommendation, aiming to discover temporal features in user interaction sequences for precise intent capture, constitutes a critical task in personalized recommendation systems. Although recent progress in Large Language Models (LLMs) has showcased remarkable capabilities in semantic understanding and logical reasoning, thereby offering new potential for enhancing recommendation quality, most existing LLM-based approaches typically focus on leveraging semantic content while directly ignoring the fine-grained temporal features inherent in interaction sequences. How to collaboratively model fine-grained temporal intervals and semantic content within a unified LLM framework remains a challenging problem. To address these limitations, we propose Time-interval-aware prompting for Recommendation with LLMs (TipRec) framework. Specifically, we first standardize item description texts to generate initial semantic representations and expand the LLM vocabulary by establishing associations between item IDs and semantic information. Concurrently, we discretize inter-event time intervals into interval tokens, which are then combined with item ID tokens to form structured sequences preserving behavioral order and temporal granularity. To facilitate the integration of temporal reasoning with deep semantic understanding, we design four collaborative fine-tuning tasks to internalize both sequential behavioral features and item-level semantics within a unified representation space. Extensive experiments conducted on four real-world datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of TipRec.
