AB  - Segmentation of medical images is essential in the diagnosis of abnormal areas and is not easy in computer-aided diagnostic systems because of the similarity and differences inherent in the images. A solution to this is provided through self-supervised learning that utilizes unlabelled data to train feature representations without annotations. Smart healthcare systems need to have highly lightweight, real-time-based segmentation models across the edge-based device. In order to resolve these challenges, this paper puts forward TransEdgeNet, which is a light self-supervised hybrid network that is used to segment medical real-time images. This paper applies the pre-processing stage in the first instance, the resizing and normalization method to improve the performance of segmentation. The proposed architecture is then an Atrous Separable Convolution-based DeepLabV3+ (ASC-DeepLabV3+) network that is trained with an Edge Sparse Vision Transformer (ESViT). TransEdgeNet uses separable convolutions, Atrous convolutions, token pruning and temporal-spatial attention mechanisms to maximize the speed of the computation and performance of the segmentation. Lastly, experimental tests based on benchmark medical imaging datasets, i.e., ISIC-2017, ISIC-2018, and PH2 show that the model can reach high segmentation accuracy of 99%, 99.02% and 98.95%, as confirmed by Dice Coefficient, Intersection over Union (IoU), and Hausdorff Distance measures.
