KW  - Privacy-preserving APT detection
KW  - Semantic evidence units
KW  - Semantic hypergraph
KW  - Evidence-level interpretability
KW  - Critical information infrastructure
AB  - Advanced persistent threat (APT) detection methods typically rely on raw-data analysis, but in privacy-constrained critical information infrastructure (CII) environments, raw-data sharing may expose sensitive identifiers, addresses, paths, and contextual details. A core limitation of existing methods is the lack of a shareable intermediate representation that retains behavior-level attack semantics and collaborative structure while reducing the exposure and practical recoverability of fine-grained raw information. To address this problem, we propose SEDetector, a privacy-preserving APT detection framework centered on Semantic Evidence Units (SEUs), which serve as the primary shareable intermediate representation. SEDetector first constructs local Behavioral Structure Slices and then maps raw behavioral fragments into SEUs through a many-to-many semantic abstraction process that removes or generalizes fine-grained raw attributes while retaining action categories, role relations, ordering cues, and selected structural continuity. Built on SEUs, SEDetector constructs an SEU-induced semantic hypergraph in which each SEU forms a hyperedge over abstract semantic tokens and, where required, opaque dataset-provided pseudonymous aliases, enabling higher-order collaborative behavior modeling and evidence-level interpretation. Across five random seeds, SEDetector achieves the highest mean Macro-F1 among the compared methods on OpTC, TCE5, and DAPT2020, reaching 99.52±0.07%, 91.15±1.29%, and 76.94±7.17%, respectively. The gains over the strongest competing baselines are statistically significant on OpTC and TCE5, whereas the 5.48-percentage-point mean gain on DAPT2020 does not reach statistical significance. Controlled low-prevalence tests and residual privacy-risk analyses further show non-trivial discrimination under severe imbalance and lower strongest-attacker Macro-F1 on SEU representations than on raw representations, although reconstruction, pseudonymous-host inference, and above-random session-linkage risks remain. Overall, the results support SEUs as a practically useful intermediate representation that incorporates privacy-sensitive information protection as a core design objective and reduces raw-information exposure and practical recoverability under the specified threat model.
